### **Setting Up HTTPS on Apache with SSL/TLS Certificates**

I began by ensuring that I had a running **Apache** server and that my domain was correctly pointing to the server. In my case, the domain was `nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be`, with a subdomain `supersecure.nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be`.

I had root access to the server and followed the steps to install **Apache**, enable SSL, and use **Certbot** to automatically manage my SSL/TLS certificates. Here's the step-by-step process I followed:

---

### **Step 1: Install Apache and SSL/TLS Dependencies**

First, I needed to install **Apache** along with the SSL dependencies. To do this, I ran:

```bash
bash
Copy code
sudo apt update
sudo apt install apache2 ssl-cert

```

Once that was done, I started the Apache server with:

```bash
bash
Copy code
sudo systemctl start apache2

```

I verified that Apache was running by accessing the server through HTTP.

---

### **Step 2: Install Certbot to Manage SSL Certificates**

Next, I installed **Certbot**, which would help me request SSL certificates and configure Apache for HTTPS. To install Certbot, I used the following command:

```bash
bash
Copy code
sudo apt install certbot python3-certbot-apache

```

Certbot is a tool that automates the process of obtaining and renewing SSL certificates.

---

### **Step 3: Obtain SSL/TLS Certificates with Certbot**

To obtain the SSL certificates for my domain, I ran this command:

```bash
bash
Copy code
sudo certbot --apache -d nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be -d secure.nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be -d supersecure.nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be

```

Certbot requested certificates for the listed domains. After I answered a few prompts (email for notifications and agreement to terms), it automatically configured Apache to use the newly generated SSL certificates and set up HTTP-to-HTTPS redirection.

---

### **Step 4: Verify SSL Installation**

After Certbot finished, I checked if the SSL certificates were installed correctly. To do this, I ran the following command:

```bash
bash
Copy code
curl -I https://supersecure.nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be

```

The response showed that SSL was correctly enabled and the server was responding with HTTPS headers.

If you get a successful response, you're all set!

---

### **Step 5: Redirect HTTP to HTTPS**

I wanted to ensure that all traffic to my domain would automatically use **HTTPS**. To do this, I edited the **HTTP** virtual host configuration to include a redirect rule.

I opened the file `/etc/apache2/sites-available/000-default.conf` with the following command:

```bash
bash
Copy code
sudo nano /etc/apache2/sites-available/000-default.conf

```

I added the following lines to set up the redirect from HTTP to HTTPS:

```
<VirtualHost *:80>
        # The ServerName directive sets the request scheme, hostname and port that
        # the server uses to identify itself. This is used when creating
        # redirection URLs. In the context of virtual hosts, the ServerName
        # specifies what hostname must appear in the request's Host: header to
        # match this virtual host. For the default virtual host (this file) this
        # value is not decisive as it is used as a last resort host regardless.
        # However, you must set it for any further virtual host explicitly.
        #ServerName www.example.com

        ServerName www.nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be
        ServerAlias nick-maharjan.sasm.uclllabs.be
          <Directory /var/www/html>
        AllowOverride All
            </Directory>

        ServerAdmin webmaster@localhost
        DocumentRoot /var/www/html

        # Available loglevels: trace8, ..., trace1, debug, info, notice, warn,
        # error, crit, alert, emerg.
        # It is also possible to configure the loglevel for particular
        # modules, e.g.
        #LogLevel info ssl:warn

        ErrorLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/error.log
        CustomLog ${APACHE_LOG_DIR}/access.log combined

        # For most configuration files from conf-available/, which are
        # enabled or disabled at a global level, it is possible to
        # include a line for only one particular virtual host. For example the
